      "content": "Sure! The main drivers of wealth inequality in the US over the past four decades include a combination of increasing income disparity, tax policies favoring the wealthy, globalization, technological advancements, and changes in economic policies, such as deregulation. Rising income inequality has been driven by a concentration of earnings at the top, with those in higher income brackets benefiting disproportionately from economic growth. Tax policies and political lobbying play a crucial role, as they often result in reductions in tax rates for high earners and capital gains while cutting benefits for lower-income households. Globalization has facilitated income mobility but also led to job losses due to offshoring. Technological changes have increased productivity but also contributed to job polarization, benefiting high-skilled, well-educated workers while leaving behind those without such qualifications. Deliberate or inadvertent economic policy shifts have further shaped the distribution of wealth.",
